---

## 1. IMPORTANT LOCATION DECISION BEFORE FIRESTORE CREATION

Production Firestore has NOT been created yet.

Before creating it, explicitly evaluate:

```text
us-central1 — Iowa
```

versus:

```text
africa-south1 — Johannesburg
```

Cloud Firestore supports `africa-south1`.

Firebase Cloud Functions 2nd Gen also supports `africa-south1`.

If the application's primary expected users are in Kenya/East Africa and there is no existing infrastructure constraint requiring Iowa, strongly evaluate colocating:

```text
Firestore: africa-south1
Functions: africa-south1
```

for lower regional latency.

If retaining:

```text
Firestore: us-central1
Functions: us-central1
```

document the concrete reason.

DO NOT create Firestore until this decision is made.

Remember:

Firestore location cannot simply be changed after provisioning.

Record the decision in:

```text
docs/FIREBASE_NATIVE_ARCHITECTURE.md
```

---

# 2. FIRESTORE CREATION DOES NOT DEPEND ON WEB APP REGISTRATION

Determine whether the authenticated Firebase/Google account has permission to create Firestore.

Check existing databases first.

Use appropriate tooling such as:

```bash
firebase firestore:databases:list
```

or:

```bash
gcloud firestore databases list \
  --project=family-tree-a4c4f
```

Use whichever installed tooling actually supports the action.

If no `(default)` Firestore database exists, create it only after the region decision has been explicitly confirmed.

Use:

```text
Standard edition
Firestore Native mode
database ID: (default)
```

Do not create a MongoDB-compatible Firestore database.

Do not use Test Mode rules.

Immediately deploy the already-developed secure rules after database creation.

